<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    The macOS bundle's Info.plist (KON-360).

    vpk can generate one, and did until now, but it derives CFBundleShortVersionString from the pack
    version and pads it to four components: 0.4.0-nightly.65 arrived as 0.4.0.0, where Apple allows at
    most three integers. There is no flag for that, so the bundle takes its plist from here instead.

    The workflow fills in three values before packing — CFBundleExecutable from the build matrix, and
    the two version keys — so nothing here has to be kept in step with a release by hand. The
    placeholders below are never shipped; the step fails the build if the version it derives is not
    one to three integers.

    CFBundleIconFile must match the file name passed to `vpk --icon`, which vpk copies into
    Contents/Resources under its own name. It is icon.icns because that is what the icon step builds;
    change one and the other stops finding it, and a bundle with a missing icon still packages fine.

    The bundle id lives here rather than on the command line because --plist and --bundleId are
    mutually exclusive. The reasoning behind the value is unchanged from KON-348: app.kontena.Kontena
    is the domain this product actually has, and it is already what MacKeychainStore stores secrets
    under and what Autostart names its LaunchAgent.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
  <string>Kontena.App</string>
  <key>CFBundleIconFile</key>
  <string>icon.icns</string>
  <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
  <string>app.kontena.Kontena</string>
  <key>CFBundleName</key>
  <string>Kontena</string>
  <key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
  <string>APPL</string>
  <key>CFBundleSignature</key>
  <string>????</string>
  <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
  <string>0.0.0</string>
  <key>CFBundleVersion</key>
  <string>0.0.0</string>
  <key>NSHighResolutionCapable</key>
  <true/>
  <key>NSPrincipalClass</key>
  <string>NSApplication</string>
</dict>
</plist>
